Here is a practical code summary for handling query parameters and slugs in an ICP on-chain frontend, based on the knowledge sources:

---

### 1. `_redirects` — SPA catch-all rewrite

Place this file at the root of your `dist/` (or build output) directory:

```
# Serve the app shell for every unmatched path (SPA rewrite)
/*  /index.html  200
```

This makes `/channels`, `/channels/world`, and `/channels?id=world` all serve `index.html`, letting your client-side router take over. [[SPA Routing](https://docs.internetcomputer.org/guides/frontends/static-site/routing/#single-page-apps-spa)]

---

### 2. Optional: give real 404s to missing assets

```
# Return a real 404 for missing build assets, then fall back to the shell
/assets/*  /404.html    404
/*         /index.html  200
```

[[SPA Routing](https://docs.internetcomputer.org/guides/frontends/static-site/routing/#single-page-apps-spa)]

---

### 3. Client-side: read the query param or slug in JS

```js
// Query param: /channels?id=world
const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
const id = params.get("id"); // "world"

// Path slug: /channels/world
const slug = window.location.pathname.split("/").pop(); // "world"
```

---

### Key rules to remember

- Always use **absolute asset paths** (`/assets/app.js`, not `assets/app.js`) — relative paths break under a `/*` rewrite. [[SPA Routing](https://docs.internetcomputer.org/guides/frontends/static-site/routing/#single-page-apps-spa)]
- Dynamic server-side captures like `/channels/:id` are **not supported** — all routing logic must live in the client. [[Dynamic Rules](https://docs.internetcomputer.org/guides/frontends/static-site/redirects/#what-isnt-supported-dynamic-rules)]



## Query Parameters and URL Slugs in ICP On-Chain Frontends

Yes, the Internet Computer's on-chain frontend (asset canister / static site canister) **does support query parameters** like `?id=world` in URLs — but with an important distinction about *where* the handling happens.

### Query Parameters (e.g. `?id=world`)

The HTTP Gateway passes the full URL, including query parameters, to the canister. From the HTTP request encoding spec:

> The `url` field contains the URL from the HTTP request line, i.e. without protocol or hostname, and **includes query parameters**.

[[HTTP Request Encoding](https://docs.internetcomputer.org/references/http-gateway-protocol-spec/#http-request-encoding)]

So a request to `http://localhost:3000/channels?id=world` will have `url = "/channels?id=world"` passed to the canister. However, the **static asset canister does not route based on query parameters** — it serves static files. Query parameters are typically read and handled **client-side** by your JavaScript app.

### Path Slugs (e.g. `/channels/world`)

For path-based routing (slugs like `/channels/world`), the asset canister supports **SPA routing** via a `_redirects` rule:

```
/*  /index.html  200
```

This rewrites any unmatched path to your app shell, letting your client-side router (React Router, Vue Router, etc.) handle the slug. [[SPA Routing](https://docs.internetcomputer.org/guides/frontends/static-site/routing/#single-page-apps-spa)]

### What is NOT supported

**Dynamic `:placeholder` or `:splat` captures** in redirect rules are not supported. You cannot write `/channels/:id /channels/detail 301` to capture and forward a slug server-side. This is because every response must be certified ahead of time, and dynamically constructed responses cannot be certified. [[Dynamic Rules](https://docs.internetcomputer.org/guides/frontends/static-site/redirects/#what-isnt-supported-dynamic-rules)]

### Summary

| Feature | Supported? | Where handled |
|---|---|---|
| `?id=world` query params | ✅ Yes | Client-side JS |
| `/channels/world` path slugs | ✅ Yes (via SPA `/*` rewrite) | Client-side router |
| Server-side dynamic routing by param/slug | ❌ No | N/A |

For a URL like `http://localhost:3000/channels?id=world`, add the `/* /index.html 200` rule and let your frontend JS read `window.location.search` to get the `id` parameter.## Query Parameters and URL Slugs in ICP On-Chain Frontends

